%I A398140 #20 Aug 06 2026 08:42:00
%S A398140 1,2,3,4,5,7,63
%N A398140 Numbers m such that the distinct prime factors of the product m*(m+1)*(m+2)*(m+3) are all consecutive primes beginning with 2 (that is, the product is a term of A055932).
%C A398140 Call a positive integer prime-complete if its distinct prime factors are consecutive primes beginning with 2. This sequence logically follows sequence A141399 of numbers, v, with prime-complete products, v*(v+1), and A398139 for numbers, w, with prime-complete products w*(w+1)*(w+2).
%C A398140 Let M_4(m) = m*(m+1)*(m+2)*(m+3) and define v = m*(m+3)/2. The number v is an integer because one of m and m+3 is even. Also, v+1 = (m+1)*(m+2)/2. Therefore M_4(m) = 4*v*(v+1).
%C A398140 Since v and v+1 are consecutive integers, v*(v+1) is even. Multiplication by 4 therefore introduces no new distinct prime factors, and hence rad(M_4(m)) = rad(v*(v+1)). Consequently, m is a term of this sequence iff m*(m+3)/2 is a term of A141399. Thus this sequence is the inverse image of A141399 under the map m -> m*(m+3)/2; its terms are obtained from those terms, v, of A141399 for which 8*v+9 is a square, by setting m = (sqrt(8*v+9)-3)/2.
%C A398140 The products, M_4, match the terms of A217056, although A217056 is defined for "highly composite numbers".
%C A398140 Conjecture: There are no more terms in this sequence past 63. This conjecture is based on analysis of the growth rate of the minimum prime index values of the GPF of M_4(m) (call it iota_min(M_4(m))) in primorial intervals, such that P_r <= M_4(m) < P_(r+1). For intervals r=3 through r=27 the iota_min progression is: 3, 3, 4, 6, 7, 6, 8, 9, 11, 13, 14, 14, 21, 23, 27, 29, 33, 38, 31, 38, 62, 63, 72, 81, 73. The significance of iota_min in these intervals is that the maximum value of omega for M_4(m) in these intervals is r, so if iota_min exceeds r, it is impossible for there to be a prime-complete product in the interval. In the sequence above, iota_min exceeds r for r=15 and above. The links to the program and output data for this analysis are below.
%H A398140 Ken Clements, <a href="https://github.com/kenatiod/Delta_min/blob/main/Delta_min_N4/Primorial_Intervals_N4.py">Program to calculate iota_min in primorial intervals</a>.
%H A398140 Ken Clements, <a href="https://github.com/kenatiod/Delta_min/blob/main/Delta_min_N4/Primorial_Intervals_N4_Output.txt">Output of iota_min program</a>.
%F A398140 For m>=1, a(n) = m for the next n, if m*(m+3)/2 is in A141399.
%e A398140 a(6) = 7 because 7*(7+3)/2 = 35, and 35 is a term of A141399. Correspondingly, 7*8*9*10 = 5040, which is A217056(6).
%e A398140 a(7) = 63 because 63*(63+3)/2 = 2079, and 2079 is a term of A141399. Correspondingly, 63*64*65*66 = 17297280, which is A217056(7).
%e A398140 The number 6 is not a term because 6*(6+3)/2 = 27 is not a term of A141399. Equivalently, 6*7*8*9 = 3024 = 2^4 * 3^3 * 7 has 7 as a prime factor but does not have 5 as a prime factor.
%t A398140 Select[Range[2^12], Or[IntegerQ@ Log2[#], And[EvenQ[#], Union@ Differences@ PrimePi@ FactorInteger[#][[All, 1]] == {1}]] &[#*(# + 1)*(# + 2)*(# + 3)] &] (* _Michael De Vlieger_, Jul 30 2026 *)
%o A398140 (Python)
%o A398140 from sympy import prime, primefactors
%o A398140 def is_prime_complete(n):
%o A398140     factors = primefactors(n)
%o A398140     return factors[-1] == prime(len(factors))
%o A398140 def is_A141399_term(v):
%o A398140     return is_prime_complete(v * (v + 1))
%o A398140 def terms(limit):
%o A398140     return [m for m in range(1, limit + 1) if is_A141399_term(m * (m + 3) // 2)]
%o A398140 print(terms(100))
%Y A398140 Cf. A141399, A217056, A055932, A007947, A052762, A398139.
%K A398140 nonn,more
%O A398140 1,2
%A A398140 _Ken Clements_, Jul 21 2026
